      <memberdef kind="variable" id="struct_a_g_remap_core_1_1_w_w_m_i_parser_config_1a04156e1f7970553bb1c5ed5c958895aa" prot="public" static="no" mutable="no">
        <type>std::vector&lt; <ref refid="struct_a_g_remap_core_1_1_skeleton_numbering_probe_1_1_numbering" kindref="compound">SkeletonNumberingProbe::Numbering</ref> &gt;</type>
        <definition>std::vector&lt;SkeletonNumberingProbe::Numbering&gt; AGRemapCore::WWMIParserConfig::skeletonNumberings</definition>
        <argsstring></argsstring>
        <name>skeletonNumberings</name>
        <qualifiedname>AGRemapCore::WWMIParserConfig::skeletonNumberings</qualifiedname>
        <briefdescription>
        </briefdescription>
        <detaileddescription>
<para><verbatim>embed:rst 
Every numbering this character&apos;s merged skeleton has had, when a game update RENUMBERED it --
the parser asks the mod&apos;s own geometry which one its bone ids are in
(:cpp:class:`SkeletonNumberingProbe`) and records that numbering&apos;s version as the mod&apos;s
:cpp:func:`IniParseData::blendVersion` :raw-html:`&lt;br /&gt;` :raw-html:`&lt;br /&gt;`

A mod&apos;s ``vb0`` cannot say: community hash-update tools rewrite a mod&apos;s hashes to the new
version and leave its bone ids alone, so one of Lynae&apos;s 3.7-``vb0`` mods carries her 3.6
numbering. Not asked of a mod from before WWMI&apos;s merged skeleton (no ``vg_offset`` in any slot
section), whose ids are each component&apos;s own. Fewer than two numberings (the default) switches
the probe off
</verbatim> </para>
        </detaileddescription>
        <inbodydescription>
        </inbodydescription>
